  Entitlements for a macOS build with the "vm" cargo feature enabled.

  The vm runtime boots a microVM through libkrun, which uses Apple's
  Hypervisor.framework. macOS refuses that to any process whose signature
  lacks com.apple.security.hypervisor, so a binary signed without these
  entitlements fails at VM start rather than at build time.

  The other two matter only under the hardened runtime, which notarized
  release binaries are signed with:

  - disable-library-validation lets the process load libkrun, which Homebrew
    signed with a different Team ID. Without it the binary does not start at
    all, since libkrun is linked rather than opened on demand.

  - allow-dyld-environment-variables keeps DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH from being
    stripped. libkrun opens its kernel, libkrunfw, by name at run time, and
    nothing else points it at Homebrew's directory.

  Builds without the "vm" feature do not need this file.

  Note: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so flag names are
  spelled without their leading dashes above.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.hypervisor</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
